## This is a GLASS HOUSE — the enclosure is yours
The place must read enclosed in glass from every authored camera and be lit by what comes through it
(the frame gate's luminance band).  Name the shell and lid meshes `RoomShell…` / `Ceiling…` so the
overview rig can lift the lid, and cut the plan's doors as openings.  `room_shell(ctx.collection,
frame_mat, center=…, extents=…, thickness=0.2, openings=(…), glazed=True)` from `lib/architecture.py`
builds an iron-frame box with glass panes on the bounds' faces if that is the brief's shape; other
shapes are yours to build.
{% elif interior %}
## This is an INTERIOR — the enclosure is yours
An interior must read enclosed from every authored camera (measured 2026-09-07 over six interior runs
with no owner for walls and roof: every one was judged "not enclosed, a diorama on a flat plane",
0.0–0.3) and be LIT INSIDE (the frame gate's luminance band; the sun is outside, so a closed room
without openings and lights reads black).  Name the lid meshes `RoomShell…` / `Ceiling…` (the overview
rig lifts the lid by name); windows and doors are openings the zones glaze or frame.
`room_shell(ctx.collection, wall_mat, center=…, extents=…, thickness=0.2, openings=(…))` from
`lib/architecture.py` is the box option (an opening = face, centre along the wall / up from the floor,
size, metres); the room's shape and extent are the brief's.
