### The harness assembles `{{ entry_file }}` and runs YOUR module like this — nothing else runs
```python
ctx.collection = scene root                      # build_env links into it
out = env.build_env(ctx)                         # ONE argument, and it is ctx
ctx.env = out if isinstance(out, dict) else {}   # what the zones read as ctx.env
ctx.height_at = env.height_at                    # THE ground every zone seats on
```
So `{{ env_file }}` must define exactly:
- `def height_at(x, y) -> z` — the ground every zone seats on, deterministic and cheap; any walkable
  surface the brief's place has (a path, a terrace, a pond basin, a floor) is part of it, so what stands
  there stands on it.  Under every plan camera position the ground stays below the camera (gate
  `camera_underground`, cap 0.50); otherwise the landform is the brief's — a floor, a slope, a basin, a
  street, a cliff are all valid.
- `def build_env(ctx) -> dict` — builds the world (sky), the scene's sun or moon lamp if the brief has
  one, the ground mesh (sampled from `height_at`, named `Ground…`), the atmosphere the probe requires (a
  volume) and whatever continues the world past the content (far geometry named `Sky…` so the census files
  it as backdrop) into `ctx.collection`, and RETURNS the dict zones read as `ctx.env` — e.g.
  `{"ground_material": …, "sun_direction": (x, y, z), "water_level": …}`; the keys are yours to name.
- Module-level names zones may `from env import …` (the skeleton's `SUN_AZIMUTH_DEG` / `SUN_ELEVATION_DEG`,
  compass 0 = +X / 90 = +Y, are one example).
The world (`bpy.data.worlds.new`, `ctx.scene.world =`) is YOURS and nobody else's: a zone that builds one
fails the lint.  No cameras (the plan's), no `scene.render.*`, no frame changes.

## What this module is graded on (the requirement, and how it is measured)
1. **The ground reads as the brief's material**, not one flat untextured colour (judge `flat_ground`: no
   blending, no wear, undressed seams).  Paving, snow, sand, decking, mud, a tiled floor all count; a
   height / slope / noise colour blend (`lib.terrain.ground` + `lib.materials.mineral_surface`) is one tool
   among others.
2. **The world does not end in view.**  From no authored camera may the ground stop against the sky or a
   backdrop float (judge `world_edge_visible`; the frame census reports "terrain ends N m ahead").  Two
   gates measure it outdoors: `world_edge` (standing geometry must reach past 1.25 × the bounds'
   half-extent) and `fog_short` (the census reads the volume's density: 1 / density has to stay ≥ span / 1.5).  What
   continues the world is the brief's place — a street, a sea, a cliff, a forest, walls, hills on one side
   and open sky on another; far geometry is named `Sky…`, standing geometry is seated, and nothing is
   pre-tinted toward the fog (it reads as cardboard: judge `placeholder_material`).
3. **No plan camera below the ground** (`camera_underground`, cap 0.50): `height_at` under every camera
   position stays below its z.  A ground feature must not read as a plate laid on the terrain from the
   overview cameras (judge `flat_ground`): carving it into `height_at` or giving it a built edge both work.
4. **Every authored frame lands in the exposure band** at the brief's time of day (`dark_frame`: mean
   luminance < 0.12 or > 35 % near-black, cap 0.55; blown frames likewise) and the light is not monochrome
   (judged).  Cycles is physical.  Starting values that have passed on a mid-albedo ground: a Sun of
   1–5 W/m² (a moon 0.05–0.3), a world (`lib.sky.sun_world`, or a deep colour for night) at strength
   0.1–0.6, fog density 0.003–0.008 (`lib.sky.haze`); a brief lit by fire, neon, storm or nothing sets
   its own and reads `camera_checks.mean_lum`.  Exposure is fixed at 0.
5. **Shadows read and shade carries colour** (judge `unlit_or_blown`, `lighting_atmosphere`: "flat
   shadows", "black shade"; the gate's `dark_frac`): a Sun `angle` of 1–3° with the sky world as fill is one
   way; any fill lamp you add is yours to justify by the frame table.
6. No {% if not (textures is defined and textures) %}image textures, no {% endif %}file IO, no unseeded randomness (`ctx.rng(seed)`); the lint warns on `bpy.ops.mesh.primitive_*` in a loop.

## DON'T
- No assets or zones here (they come from other modules).  No cameras.  No `scene.*` render / view settings.
- A route the zones must agree with (a lane, a towpath) is part of `height_at`, or a module-level name
  here that zones import — the one ground authority; the name is yours.

